# An editor wears the rail too — in its gutter, so nothing moves.
#
# A terminal's grid owns its first column, so its body starts one cell
# in. An editor's does not: the gutter opens with the sign column, which
# is blank unless a diagnostic or a decoration is on that line, so the
# rail goes in that cell (`pane_rail.drawOver`). The line numbers and
# the text stay on the screen column they were on — the row below reads
# `▌  1 fn main…`, the rail and then the gutter exactly as it was.
command view.toggle_tree
write main.rs "fn main() {}\nlet x = 1;\n"
open main.rs
expect screen contains "fn main"

# The rail in the gutter's first cell, the number right behind it.
expect screen contains "▌  1 fn main"
expect screen contains "▌  2 let x = 1;"
# In a palette colour — the first pane open takes the ladder's green.
expect color 0 2 fg #98c379
